Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.719 --> 00:00:03.590 align:start position:0%
 
hi this is prashta walker  this math problem was so hard it caused  an uproar over social media and had some  scottish students in tears  a crocodile is stalking a zebra that is  20 meters upstream on the opposite side  of a river  crocodiles travel at different speeds on  land and in water  the crocodile can stalk the prey faster  if it swims to a point p that is x  meters upstream on the other side of the  river  the time  taken in tenths of a second is given by  the following equation  part one calculate the time if the  crocodile does not travel on land  part two calculate the time if the  crocodile swims the shortest distance  possible  and part three calculate the shortest  time and the corresponding value of x  can you solve it  give this puzzle a try when you're ready  keep watching the video i'm going to  present the solution  and at the very end for part three i'm  going to present a solution they  probably would not have accepted but  it's what i would consider to be the  elegant solution and it actually can  help you solve many problems like this  so let's tackle the first part  calculate the time of the crocodile does  not travel on land  this means the crocodile travels  entirely in the water  this means the crocodile  is traveling all the way across  point p is all the way by the zebra  and so that means x is equal to 20.  we can substitute that value into the  equation for time  and find that the time taken is  approximately 104 tenths of a second  part two  calculate the time if the crocodile  swims the shortest distance possible  i was a little confused by the wording  the question what they mean is calculate  the time of the crocodile takes the  least amount of distance in the water  so that happens if the crocodile goes  directly across the water because any  other path the crocodile takes is going  to be a longer distance in the water  so basically the crocodile is traveling  all 20 meters  on land  so in this case x goes all the way x  meters upstream is zero  so the time will be equal  to 110 tenths of a second  part three calculate the shortest time  and the corresponding value of x  this problem is about calculus  and the standard way to solve this is  you take the derivative of the time and  set it equal to zero  you need to remember to use the chain  rule you want to take one half times 36  plus x squared raised to the power of  negative one-half and then remember to  multiply by 2x  if you can do that and set up this  equation  it requires a little bit of manipulation  but you'll eventually get that x equals  8 and the time is 98 tenths of a second  so this is probably the way they  expected students to solve it on the pro  on the exam  but there's a much more elegant way  and it actually brings into physics  so the first thing you need to do is  actually you need to reverse engineer  some of the question they've actually  given you the time it takes for the  crocodile to get to the zebra  what they've left out is some of the  background we actually don't usually  know  this kind of neat equation what we  usually get  is we just have to figure out all right  if you're traveling x meters upstream  that means the distance you're traveling  on land is 20 minus x so if you look at  the equation for time the 20 minus x  appears in the second part  so it's being multiplied by 4 because  that refers to the speed of the  crocodile  so we can kind of deduce the crocodile  must have a land speed of 1 4  meters per second  similarly if we look at the other part  of the equation we can see that 5 must  refer to the water speed  and in order to have that 36 plus x  squared where is that square root coming  from  this is because we have a hypotenuse  which must be the square root of 36 plus  x squared  and here we have six so there's a right  triangle form between the distance the  crocodile travels in the water the  amount x that travels upstream and we  can deduce the distance across the river  must be 6 in order for the hypotenuse to  be the square root of 36 plus x squared  so why are we reverse engineering this  you'll see in a second  so i'm going to get rid of some of the  problems and we're just going to make a  kind of physics  uh  analogy so right here  let's consider the path the crocodile  takes in the water and then the path the  crocodile takes on land so you could  think about this as light traveling  through two different mediums so imagine  light is traveling through water and  then it's going on land or you could  have you know maybe there's a classic  problem where a swimmer is running on  sand and then going into the ocean and  swimming and there are different rates  in these two mediums  so we set up these right triangles  and we have angles theta 1 and theta 2  from  the separation between the two mediums  the two the water and the land  and there's something called snell's law  which tells you a relation between these  angles  sine of theta one divided by sine of  theta 2  is always equal to the ratio of the  velocities in the two mediums  so where are the velocities coming from  in this case  we need to figure out this is why we  figured out what the land and the water  speed was  we actually have a special case before i  get to that in this case theta 2 you can  see is a 90 degree angle once you get to  land you're going straight  and so that means  this simplifies into sine of theta 1 is  equal to the ratio of the velocities  so what are the velocities in these two  mediums  well we had the water speed was  one-fifth and the land speed is  one-fourth  so that means the sine of theta one is  the ratio of one-fifth and one-fourth  which is four-fifth  now how does the sine of an angle ever  equal four-fifths  that only happens in a right triangle  specifically a three four five right  triangle  in our diagram we must have a three four  five right triangle  and we have the smallest leg that's  equal to six  so that means our hypotenuse is ten  and the other leg is eight because we've  doubled a three four five right triangle  and like magic we figured out that x has  to equal eight  we can substitute that back into the  equation for time and then we get time  equals ninety eight so it's a very  elegant solution if you use snell's law  this relates to optics and a bunch of  other physics problems  so this is probably not the answer they  would have wanted but it's a very cool  way to solve it  thanks for watching this video please  subscribe to my channel i make videos on  math and game theory you can catch me on  my blog mind your decisions which you  can follow on facebook google plus and  patreon you can catch me on social media  at preshtalwalkar and if you like this  video please check out my books  there's links in the video description

